1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:04,242
Imagine the whole prompt is “Fix
validation.” Which input fails?

2
00:00:04,924 --> 00:00:06,071
What result should change?

3
00:00:06,754 --> 00:00:07,931
Which files are in scope?

4
00:00:08,613 --> 00:00:12,606
If those questions are unanswered, an
agent can propose a broad refactor that

5
00:00:12,606 --> 00:00:13,553
sounds reasonable.

6
00:00:13,934 --> 00:00:17,565
Start in Copilot Plan and use the
displayed prompt as a baseline.

7
00:00:17,886 --> 00:00:19,707
Then make each missing part explicit.

8
00:00:20,713 --> 00:00:23,716
We can replace the broad request with a
precise observation.

9
00:00:23,982 --> 00:00:26,550
The order validator currently returns
true for zero,

10
00:00:26,721 --> 00:00:28,193
but the issue requires false.

11
00:00:28,654 --> 00:00:31,856
Attach the validator and the tests from
the training checkout.

12
00:00:32,367 --> 00:00:35,062
Ask Copilot to identify the condition
responsible.

13
00:00:35,513 --> 00:00:38,302
Now you can open the named file and
check the cause yourself.

14
00:00:39,399 --> 00:00:41,227
State what the change is allowed to
touch.

15
00:00:41,629 --> 00:00:45,808
Keep the existing function signature,
edit only the validator and the relevant

16
00:00:45,808 --> 00:00:50,228
test file, and preserve the behavior for
one, five, and negative one.

17
00:00:50,678 --> 00:00:53,853
That gives the agent a boundary and a
small set of examples.

18
00:00:54,234 --> 00:00:56,073
If it proposes to rename the function,

19
00:00:56,385 --> 00:00:58,605
you already know which requirement that
violates.

20
00:00:59,527 --> 00:01:01,862
Request the output you can review before
any edit.

21
00:01:02,262 --> 00:01:06,237
Ask for the cause, the files it would
touch, the smallest proposed change,

22
00:01:06,588 --> 00:01:08,460
and one test that should expose the bug.

23
00:01:08,970 --> 00:01:12,257
Have it ask about missing requirements
and stop after the plan.

24
00:01:12,558 --> 00:01:16,465
Then look for the line that explains why
a test for zero should fail on the

25
00:01:16,465 --> 00:01:17,182
original code.

26
00:01:18,151 --> 00:01:20,592
Suppose the first plan introduces a new
parameter.

27
00:01:20,994 --> 00:01:23,509
Tell Copilot which part conflicts with
the issue.

28
00:01:23,789 --> 00:01:28,407
“Preserve the existing signature. Revise
only the comparison and the zero

29
00:01:28,407 --> 00:01:28,953
regression.”

30
00:01:29,314 --> 00:01:33,698
Check the updated plan for both points.
A precise correction lets you tell

31
00:01:33,889 --> 00:01:36,567
whether your next message fixed the
misunderstanding.

32
00:01:37,545 --> 00:01:41,556
Add a line about evidence. Run the same
test command in the approved runner

33
00:01:41,556 --> 00:01:43,177
before and after the fix.

34
00:01:43,749 --> 00:01:47,372
Before the fix, the zero assertion
should fail for the reported reason.

35
00:01:47,793 --> 00:01:50,051
After it, the new and existing tests
should pass.

36
00:01:50,553 --> 00:01:53,798
Ask for the observed output and check it
against the requirement.

37
00:01:54,330 --> 00:01:56,488
That makes the task's definition of done
visible.

38
00:01:57,463 --> 00:02:00,262
Pick another synthetic issue and write
four short lines.

39
00:02:00,643 --> 00:02:04,027
Say what happens, what may change, what
the assistant should produce,

40
00:02:04,359 --> 00:02:05,795
and how you will verify it.

41
00:02:06,076 --> 00:02:08,474
Read them to a teammate without any
other context.

42
00:02:08,965 --> 00:02:11,648
If they can describe the expected change
and the test,

43
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:13,256
your brief is ready to use.
